ank: Count', e.g., '2: 4' means there are four 2s left unknown)\n10: 1, 8: 2, 6: 1, 5: 1, 4: 1, 3: 2\nValid Moves:\nsingle: ♥6\nsingle: ♦7\n\nSelect the best move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Evaluation: This is a free play situation (no previous play to beat), so I can choose any valid combination from my hand. My available legal moves are limited to playing a single card: either ♥6 or ♦7. Since I only have two cards and cannot form any combination with them, I must play one of these singles.\n\nMy teammate (Next Player) has 7 cards left, while the landlord (Prev Player) has just 1 card — this is critical. The landlord is very close to winning; they will win on their next turn unless we intervene. However, since it\'s currently my turn and I cannot empty my hand in one move (I have two cards), I must consider how to best help my teammate gain control.\n\nIf I play a low card now (say, ♥6), I leave my stronger card ♦7 for later. But if I play ♦7 now, I keep ♥6 — which is slightly lower. There’s minimal strategic difference between the two in rank, but considering card distribution, the remaining unknown cards include one 6 and one 5 — suggesting higher small cards like 7 might be more powerful later.\n\nBut crucially, **since the landlord has only 1 card**, they will likely win the trick unless my teammate can overtake after me. However, my teammate just passed previously, indicating possible lack of high singles. Therefore, I should **play the lower of the two singles** now to preserve a potentially useful higher card for disruption later.\n\nThus, playing **♥6** now keeps ♦7 — which is ranked higher than 6 — for the next round, possibly allowing me to respond if the landlord tries to go out with a single.\n\nReason: Play ♥6 (lower card) to retain ♦7 as a slightly higher exit card for the next round, maximizing flexibility and giving my teammate more chances to play safely. Since the landlord is at 1 card, minimizing wasted high cards is key. Playing too high now risks losing control later when coordination matters most.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♥6"]}'}
